  "Question": "Solve the equation \\(\\frac{1}{x-1}=\\frac{2}{x^2-1}\\).",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(x=1\\)",
    "B": "\\(x=3\\)",
    "C": "The original equation has no solution."
  },
  "Correct Answer": "C",
  "Accepted Answers": [],
  "Unordered Answers": false,
  "Solution": "Multiplying both sides by \\((x-1)(x+1)\\) gives \\(x+1=2\\), so \\(x=1\\). However, \\(x=1\\) makes the denominator \\(0\\), so it is an extraneous solution. Therefore, the original equation has no solution.\n\nAlternative method 1: Show that the two sides never agree\n\nSince \\(x^2-1=(x-1)(x+1)\\), the original equation is defined only for \\(x\\ne1,-1\\). Subtracting the right side from the left lets us test whether they can be equal. For every allowed \\(x\\),\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{x-1}-\\frac{2}{x^2-1}\n=\\frac{x+1-2}{(x-1)(x+1)}\n=\\frac{x-1}{(x-1)(x+1)}\n=\\frac{1}{x+1}.\n\\]\nThe cancellation is valid because \\(x\\ne1\\). The final fraction has numerator \\(1\\) and a nonzero denominator, so it cannot equal zero. Thus the two sides are never equal, and the original equation has no solution, choice C.",
